WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.322
Welcome to module one.

2
00:00:01.322 --> 00:00:10.430
In the next two minutes we will define a digital twin, separate it from things that only look like one, and see why transport is both a natural fit and a hard case.

3
00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:17.749
A digital twin has three parts: a physical system, a virtual replica of it, and the connection between them.

4
00:00:17.749 --> 00:00:21.137
Data flows in from the road, and decisions flow back out.

5
00:00:21.137 --> 00:00:24.406
Without that two-way connection, you only have a model.

6
00:00:25.306 --> 00:00:28.284
Kritzinger and colleagues give a simple test.

7
00:00:28.284 --> 00:00:30.600
A model has no automatic data flow.

8
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.437
A shadow receives live data, but people act on it by hand.

9
00:00:34.437 --> 00:00:39.400
Only when data flows automatically in both directions do we call it a twin.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.913
A dashboard answers the first question.

11
00:00:42.913 --> 00:00:49.278
A full twin climbs all five: it monitors, diagnoses, predicts, tests options, and finally acts.

12
00:00:49.278 --> 00:00:52.896
Each step needs more trust in the data and the models.

13
00:00:53.796 --> 00:00:55.778
Transport is harder than a factory.

14
00:00:55.778 --> 00:01:04.782
The system is open, full of human behaviour, spread across many owners, and its decisions run from a signal change in seconds to a new metro line over decades.

15
00:01:05.682 --> 00:01:08.105
So every good twin starts with one decision.

16
00:01:08.105 --> 00:01:14.273
The decision sets the clock, and the clock sets which data, which model detail, and which update speed you need.

17
00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:16.806
That is the thread for the rest of the course.
